<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs exclusions for the StoreOps harness gates.

  One exclusion, and it exists to resolve a contradiction between two gates rather than to quiet an
  inconvenient finding.

  ERR-1 is deliberately scoped to `service` and `web`: checkstyle-suppressions.xml exempts `domain`
  and `repository`, because "no raw throws" is a rule about the error contract the API exposes, and a
  domain entity rejecting a null id in its constructor is not an API error path. So the harness
  explicitly permits `throw new IllegalArgumentException(...)` in a domain constructor.

  SpotBugs at threshold=Medium then reports CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW on exactly that code: "Exception
  thrown in class ... will leave the constructor." BUILD-1 fails, and BUILD-2 forbids the Generator
  from adding @SuppressWarnings to make it pass. The Generator is left with no legal move — it cannot
  write a validating entity constructor, cannot silence the finding, and no gate message explains
  which rule it is supposed to obey. That is a contract asking for two incompatible things, which is
  the escalation case, triggered on ordinary correct code.

  Measured, not predicted: a four-line entity constructor that rejects a null id fails
  `mvn verify -Pharness-gates` with "Total bugs: 1" and nothing else wrong.

  The exclusion is narrow on purpose — this bug pattern, in domain packages only. CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW
  warns about a partially-constructed object being observable through a finalizer or a leaked `this`,
  which is a real concern for a non-final class with a finalizer and not a concern for an immutable
  record-like entity that validates and assigns.

  Everywhere else SpotBugs stays at Medium. Adding a second entry here is a change to what BUILD-1
  means and goes past the engineering standards team, not into a feature commit — the same standard
  checkstyle-suppressions.xml holds itself to.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW"/>
        <Or>
            <Class name="~com\.cognizant\.storeops\.[a-z]+\.domain\..*"/>
            <Class name="~com\.cognizant\.storeops\.shared\.domain\..*"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

    <!--
      Constructor injection is not an exposure of internal representation.

      EI_EXPOSE_REP2 fires when a constructor stores a reference to an externally mutable object,
      and the pattern exists to catch a value crossing an API boundary without a defensive copy -
      a Date, an array, a collection. A Spring component storing an injected collaborator in a
      final field is the opposite case: sharing that one instance is the entire point, and copying
      it would be meaningless.

      Without this entry the harness contradicts itself at every single injection point.
      coding-conventions requires "constructor injection only, no @Autowired on fields" and "final
      fields"; follow that and BUILD-1 fails; BUILD-2 then forbids the Generator from suppressing
      the finding. There is no legal move, and it is not a corner case - it is every service and
      every controller in the codebase.

      Measured on the generated StoreOps scaffold: four EI_EXPOSE_REP2 findings against
      ActivityServiceImpl.taskRepository, ActivityController.activityService,
      ProgrammeServiceImpl.projectRepository and ProgrammeController.programmeService, with
      Checkstyle clean, 95 tests green and all nine ArchUnit rules passing.

      Worth knowing: it flagged four of roughly twenty otherwise identical injection points.
      SpotBugs judges mutability from the implementations it can see, so a repository interface
      backed by a map is "mutable" and an interface whose impl holds no field is not. That
      inconsistency is the strongest argument for excluding the pattern here rather than asking
      developers to predict it - a gate nobody can predict is a gate that gets worked around.

      Scoped to constructors, and only in the layers where injection happens. Ordinary methods and
      getters keep the check, so a service that returns its own mutable collection is still caught.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
        <Method name="&lt;init&gt;"/>
        <Or>
            <Class name="~com\.cognizant\.storeops\..*\.service\..*"/>
            <Class name="~com\.cognizant\.storeops\..*\.web\..*"/>
            <Class name="~com\.cognizant\.storeops\.shared\.event\..*"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
